# Machine-specific paths and credentials for this repo. Copy to `.env` and edit.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is GITIGNORED, and must stay that way: this repo is PUBLIC, and these are
# absolute paths and credentials on your machine. Same name and format as the player
# repo's `.env`, so one block of variables can serve both (a symlink works).
#
# Format is `VAR=value` (no `export`, no quotes needed), which is both make-includable
# (the Makefile `-include`s this file) and shell-sourceable:
#
#   set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
# Every value below is an EXAMPLE. Nothing here is a real path on anyone's machine -- fill in
# your own.

# Where the ORIGINAL track recordings live -- the `NNN-Artist - Title.ext` files that
# `streamalign track-mix` and the origNNN spans in `streamalign hints` correlate against
# the mix. The repo's `sources` symlink points at an external media volume that is often
# not mounted; point this at a copy that is always there.
#
# This is also where the harvester reads its Mystery Track queries from: a clip named
# `Mystery Track <N>.<wav|mp3|flac|m4a>` puts mystery N into the search. No clip, no search --
# see PROCESS.md, "Adding a new Mystery Track clip".
NETRADIO_SOURCES_DIR=/path/to/your/sources

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube cookies (OPTIONAL -- and OFF by default, deliberately)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YouTube answers anonymous downloads with "Sign in to confirm you're not a bot." That error
# carries no 403 and no 429, so waiting does not clear it: only a signed-in session does. The
# harvester HALTS on it rather than grinding through the queue failing identically on every item.
#
# The cookie IS a credential -- it is your logged-in YouTube session. That is why the harvester
# never goes looking for one on its own, why these are unset by default, and why they live here
# in `.env` (gitignored) and never in the repo.
#
# Set ONE of the two. A cookie FILE wins over a browser if both are set.
#
# 1. A cookies.txt file (Netscape format). PREFERRED on macOS -- it is just a file, so it needs
#    no Keychain, and it will not prompt you. See PROCESS.md, "Feeding the harvester a cookie".
#NETRADIO_YTDLP_COOKIES=/path/to/youtube-cookies.txt
#
# 2. Read them straight out of a browser profile: brave|chrome|chromium|edge|firefox|opera|safari
#    (anything yt-dlp accepts, including a named profile such as `chrome:Profile 2`).
#
#    WARNING, macOS: `chrome` (and any Chromium-based browser) stores its cookies ENCRYPTED and
#    decrypts them through the login Keychain, so every single invocation raises a
#    "security wants to use your ... Chrome Safe Storage" password prompt. A harvester that
#    restarts will prompt you again, and again. `firefox` does NOT have this problem -- it keeps
#    cookies in a plain `cookies.sqlite` -- so prefer firefox, or option 1 above.
#NETRADIO_YTDLP_COOKIES_FROM_BROWSER=firefox

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The live canary (OPTIONAL)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `selftest.py`'s LIVE check proves the whole streaming pipeline still works, end to end: it finds
# a stream of a track we have already SOLVED, fetches it, and demands it score as a true match
# against our own copy of that record.
#
# It searches YouTube for the subject's name and takes the first hit -- and for the current subject
# (track 3, Jamie Myerson - Sky Blue) that hit is NOT the record: it scores 0.0867 against our own
# copy, so the guard refuses it. Correct -- a canary that cries wolf is worse than no canary -- but
# it leaves the live check permanently "not checked".
#
# Point it at a stream you know IS the record to break the deadlock. It is still validated against
# our own copy exactly like a searched one: a hand-picked URL is a HINT, never an override.
#NETRADIO_CANARY_URL=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=...

# --- Commercial music-ID APIs (scripts/identify_by_api.py) --------------------------------
# Ask ACRCloud/AudD to name a mystery clip against their ~150-160M-track catalogues (which the
# local chroma pool can't reach). NOTE: measured to be defeated by the 1998 ISDN/EQ damage the
# same way AcoustID is -- see Archive/LESSON_acoustid_stream.md. A service is used only if its
# keys are set. Real keys go in .env (gitignored), never here.
#AUDD_API_TOKEN=            # audd.io -- 300 free requests, no card
#ACRCLOUD_HOST=            # identify-<region>.acrcloud.com  (from your ACRCloud project)
#ACRCLOUD_ACCESS_KEY=
#ACRCLOUD_ACCESS_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The cache policy (scripts/cache_budget.py; OFF until NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT is set)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The tools here decode audio to float32 arrays once and cache the arrays, so a run
# that re-reads the same files skips ffmpeg. That decoded-array cache runs on the
# machine's one cache policy: one directory per cache, a cap and an age limit per
# cache, one disk floor shared by every cache, and every deletion through the
# policy. Unset, the policy is dark: nothing is registered,
# nothing is cached or evicted, and every decode runs again. Set only to depart from
# the default:
#NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT=/path/to/your/cache-root
# The disk floor, in percent full (used / (used + free)); no cache adds an entry past
# it, and an eviction run frees entries across caches until the volume is under it.
# Default 82.
#NETRADIO_DISK_MAX_PCT=82
# Days a record stays in the policy's event log (events.jsonl, under the cache root).
# Default 30.
#NETRADIO_CACHE_EVENTS_DAYS=30
# The decoded-array cache, `streamalign` — what an align run decodes once and re-reads
# many times, refilled by decoding again in seconds. Its settings, each optional and
# defaulting as shown:
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_GB            cap in GB, decimal allowed   (default 4)
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS  evict entries older than this (default none)
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_DIR           its directory ($NETRADIO_CACHE_ROOT/streamalign)
#   NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_HEADROOM_MB   cap slice a decode of known size may not
#                                           use (default 0)
# An align session is days, not months, so this machine caps the cache's age:
#NETRADIO_STREAMALIGN_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS=14
